.TH SDL_GetPreferredLocales 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_GetPreferredLocales \- Report the user\(aqs preferred locale.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_locale.h>
.PP
.BI "SDL_Locale ** SDL_GetPreferredLocales(int *count);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
This returns a NULL-terminated array of pointers to locale information\[char46] The returned list of locales are in the order of the user\(aqs preference\[char46] For example, a German citizen that is fluent in US English and knows enough Japanese to navigate around Tokyo might have a list like:

.IP
.EX
{
    { "de", "DE" },
    { "en", "US" },
    { "jp", NULL },
    NULL
}
.EE
.PP

Someone from England might prefer British English (where \(dqcolor\(dq is spelled \(dqcolour\(dq, etc), but will settle for anything like it:

.IP
.EX
{
    { "en", "GB" },
    { "en", NULL },
    NULL
}
.EE
.PP

This function returns NULL on error, including when the platform does not supply this information at all\[char46]
.PP
Note that this information is merely guidance; some platforms don\(aqt supply it, some only supply a single language ever, some don\(aqt ever provide country information, etc\[char46] Be prepared to receive surprising results and plan to have fallbacks\[char46]
.PP
This might be a \(dqslow\(dq call that has to query the operating system\[char46] It\(aqs best to ask for this once and save the results\[char46] However, this list can change, usually because the user has changed a system preference outside of your program; SDL will send an
.BR SDL_EVENT_LOCALE_CHANGED
event in this case, if possible, and you can call this function again to get an updated copy of preferred locales\[char46]
.PP
The returned pointer is a single allocation (all the strings and structures are allocated in a single chunk, even though they look like separate data), and should be disposed of with a single call to
.BR SDL_free ()
when it is no longer needed\[char46]
.PP
If not NULL,
.BR *count
will be set to number of items returned, not counting the terminating NULL pointer\[char46]
.BR count
may be NULL if one plans to simply iterate the returned array directly\[char46]

.SH FUNCTION PARAMETERS
.TP
.I count
a pointer filled in with the number of locales returned, may be NULL\[char46]
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns a NULL-terminated array of locale pointers, or NULL on failure; call
.BR SDL_GetError ()
for more information\[char46] Call
.BR SDL_free ()
when done with this pointer\[char46]

.SH THREAD SAFETY
This function is not thread safe\[char46]

.SH AVAILABILITY
This function is available since SDL 3\[char46]2\[char46]0\[char46]

